    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Choose a map $g': B \rightarrow X$ representing the homotopy class $\overline{g}$. Choose a cylinder object 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[ A \coprod A \rightarrow C(A) \rightarrow A, \]
</div>
    <p>
       and a factorization 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  C(A) \coprod _{A \coprod A} (B \coprod B) \rightarrow C(B) \rightarrow B \]
</div>
    <p>
       where the first map is a cofibration and the second a trivial fibration. We observe that $C(B)$ is a cylinder object for $B$. 
    <p>
      Since $g' \circ i$ is homotopic to $f$, there exists a map $h_0: C(A) \coprod _{A} B \rightarrow X$ with $h|B = g'$ and $h|A = f$. The inclusion $C(A) \coprod _{A} B \rightarrow C(B)$ is a trivial cofibration, so $h_0$ extends to a map $h: C(B) \rightarrow X$. We may regard $h$ as a homotopy from $g'$ to $g$, where $g \circ i = f$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
